{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 confirmed RCA (rebar-debug).\n\nProven root cause — tests/unit/test_repo_isolation_guard.py:77-106 uses aggregate nested-pytest wall time as a functional oracle for the five-second repo-isolation watchdog. The outer subprocess timeout=12 at line 97 covers nested pytest startup, the intentional five-second blocked-Git watchdog, pytest session-finish/unconfigure, and runner scheduling. It therefore leaves only about seven seconds for unrelated overhead and turns a correct inner TimeoutExpired into failure on a loaded runner. The elapsed < 10 assertion at lines 103-106 is a second instance of the same defect: an otherwise functionally correct completion between 10 and 12 seconds is rejected.\n\nOrganic evidence — Gerrit 1869 run 32187810048/job 95875806054 failed only this macOS/Python 3.13 test at 12.04 seconds, after captured stderr already showed the intended inner git status --porcelain TimeoutExpired after five seconds. The sibling head test passed at 8.74 seconds and 13,880 other tests passed. Three concurrent macOS runs of unchanged code passed this same test at 7.64, 8.03, and 9.18 seconds; local macOS runs were 6.27-6.52 seconds.\n\nDiscriminating runtime proof — temporary instrumentation, removed before this comment, recorded the inner timeout, direct shim PID liveness, pytest_unconfigure boundaries, and nested completion while varying only teardown delay / the two aggregate timing knobs:\n- delay=7, outer cap=12: OUTER_TIMEOUT at 12.008 seconds after INNER_TIMEOUT and UNCONFIGURE_START; blocked shim already dead.\n- same delay=7, cap=30: COMPLETED functionally at 12.883 seconds, nonzero result with TimeoutExpired output, shim count 2, blocked shim dead.\n- delay=5, cap=30 with elapsed<10 enforced: functional result at 11.030 seconds rejected only by ELAPSED_ASSERTION.\n- identical delay=5 without elapsed assertion: COMPLETED functionally at 10.992 seconds.\nThis rules out failure to reach the second probe, failure of the five-second watchdog, a live-child/descriptor reap hang, and generic runner infrastructure. Runner load is the trigger that expands overhead; the changeable defect is the test's tight aggregate wall-clock oracle.\n\nDefect legitimacy — defect. scripts/check_wall_clock_asserts.py:4-9 and :18-23 plus signed story faintish-bumbling-tadpole require structural/counting proof or a generous hang guard whose ceiling dwarfs expected time. The introducing callous-amused-hart report explicitly accepted the 12-second ceiling only because it should not determine functional success; this CI failure disproves that premise.\n\nCaller-dependency gate — expected post-fix behavior is that shim count, nonzero nested result, and explicit TimeoutExpired output determine watchdog success, with only a genuinely generous outer stuck-process fail-safe. The helper has only the status/sessionfinish and head/autouse tests at lines 149 and 156; neither depends on the tight aggregate deadlines. No behavioral dependency blocks repair."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787091502046966001,"uuid":"1106b1a9-51f5-4286-bc22-3e4af05fce9c"}